# WordPress Content Editing

Use this Rule when editing WordPress page, post, or options content through the REST API in a project that uses ACF Flexible Content. The invoked workflow is the bundled `content` Skill; this Rule is the safety bar for every content write, including ones started from another Skill.

## Core Model

ACF Flexible Content stores page sections as an ordered array. Each item has an `acf_fc_layout` value that identifies the layout type. The Flexible Content field name, available layout names, page IDs, and clone group keys belong in `PROJECT-CONTEXT.md`. Layout definitions live in ACF Local JSON files (`acf-local-json` Rule); content values live in the WordPress database and are mutated over REST, not as tracked theme source.

## Read Before Writing

Before changing content:

1. Confirm the target URL is the project's staging or dev site from `PROJECT-CONTEXT.md`. Stop before writes against the live URL unless the user explicitly confirmed that target.
2. Read the page, post, or options document including its ACF data (`context=edit`).
3. Identify the target block by page ID, array index, layout name, or visible copy.
4. Preserve the full Flexible Content array.
5. Change only the fields requested by the task.
6. Verify the resulting content on the target URL after a status-bridge cache flush.

## Write The Complete Array

When updating a page through REST, send the complete Flexible Content array back. Do not send only the changed block.

Generic REST shape (field name from `PROJECT-CONTEXT.md`, never hardcoded):

```http
POST /wp-json/wp/v2/pages/<page-id>
```

```json
{
  "acf": {
    "<flexible-content-field-name>": []
  }
}
```

Populate the array from the read response, then apply the intended minimal edit.

## Fields To Preserve

Do not change these unless the user explicitly asks:

- `acf_fc_layout`
- ACFE layout metadata fields
- Layout and style fields
- Image and media attachment IDs
- Button link targets, variants, and IDs
- Nested Flexible Content structure

Nested Flexible Content blocks follow the same rule: preserve the full nested array and edit only the intended fields.

## Type Normalization

REST reads may return values in a shape that differs from what writes accept. Before writing:

- Convert ACFE toggle booleans (`acfe_flexible_toggle`) to the value shape the project accepts.
- Keep enum fields on valid project-defined values.
- Use attachment IDs for media fields, not URLs. New uploads go through the `wp-media-import` Skill first.
- Preserve existing HTML patterns in rich text fields. Plain-text and HTML fields use real UTF-8 characters, not HTML entities for umlauts.

## Encoding

Send JSON as UTF-8 bytes with `Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8`. Do not round-trip the body through a mis-encoded PowerShell string.

## Out Of Scope

- ACF field-group definitions, new Flexible Content layouts, and WST templates — that is `wst-section-workflow` / `wst-new-post-type`.
- CSS/SCSS — that is `frontend-section-qa` / `cpt-frontend-qa`.
- Direct database writes and WP-CLI content writes. There is no server shell.

## Work Record Notes

If content edits affect a Section or CPT that has a work record in the project `docs/` layer, update that record with the page URL, the changed content fields, any layout/style fields deliberately preserved, and any visual behavior that still needs CSS or Playwright verification. Do not treat a content write as a frontend QA pass.
